<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ashnakkum</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.) · capital Ashnakkum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ashnakkum, a kingdom of the upper Khabur region in the age of Mari, one of the shifting players in the politics of northern Mesopotamia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ashnakkum was a city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ida-maras">Ida-Maras</a> district in north-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, often identified with Chagar Bazar, though this is debated. Chagar Bazar, excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-mallowan">Max Mallowan</a> in the 1930s, served as a provincial centre under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a> in the early 18th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a>'s death Ashnakkum became an independent kingdom that appears often in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> letters, alternately allied with and opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>. Tablets from Chagar Bazar record rations and administration of farmland, livestock and workers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ashnakkum fell under the shadow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> after the destruction of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> c. 1760 BCE and lost its independence. The region later formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ashnakkum.jpg" alt="Ashnakkum" width="320" height="199"><figcaption>Image: Vittigo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ne_syria_archaeological_sites.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="